    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1959-p38.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let \\(u=\\sqrt{2x}\\ge0.\\) Since \\(x=\\frac{u^2}{2},\\) the equation becomes \\[ 2u^2+u=1, \\] or \\((2u-1)(u+1)=0.\\) Thus \\(u=\\frac{1}{2}\\) and \\(x=\\frac{u^2}{2}=\\frac{1}{8}.\\) The other quadratic root is inadmissible because \\(u\\ge0.\\) Hence \\(x\\) is fractional.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Set \\(u=\\sqrt{2x},\\) so \\(x=\\frac{u^2}{2}\\) and \\(u\\ge0\\)",
        "Solve the resulting quadratic \\(2u^2+u-1=0\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
